      "text": "The goal is to find the longest segment in the diagram by comparing the lengths first.\nSince we have angles, we can use the law of sines to find the lengths of the sides.\nTriangle ABC is a right triangle, with hypotenuse length 55.\nThe opposite side length is 55 * sin(90 - 40) = 55 * sin(50), which is shorter than 55\nThe adjacent side length is 55 * cos(90 - 40) = 55 * cos(50), which is shorter than 55\nKnowing the lengths of the three legs of triangle ABC, we can find its perimeter and then find the ratio of the lengths of sides BD to 55, in order to find the length of BD.\n[asy]\npair A = (-3,0), B=(0,2), C=(3,0), D=(0,-1);\ndraw(D(MP(\"A\", A, W))--D(MP(\"B\", B, N))--D(MP(\"C\", C, E))--D(MP(\"D\", D, S))--A);\ndraw(B--D);\nMP(\"55^\\circ\", (0,-0.75), NW);\nMP(\"55^\\circ\", (0,-0.75), NE);\nMP(\"40^\\circ\", (0,1.5), SW);\nMP(\"75^\\circ\", (0,1.5), SE);\n[/asy]\nWe see BD is longer than 55, so it is the longest segment.\nThe final answer is 55",
