      "text": "To find the longest segment, we can refer to the given angles and their relative locations to estimate the lengths of the segments.\nThe lengths of the segments are proportional to their angle measurements.\nUsing the given information, we know that AB is 55 degrees, AD is 75 degrees, and the other two angles form a right angle with the line segments AB and AD.\nTherefore, the longest segment is the one that falls on the outside of the figure, making an angle of 180 degrees with the line segment AD.\nSince AB and AD are the shortest segments, the longest segment must fall on the side opposite the right angle in the triangle.\nThe longest segment is CD.\n\\boxed{CD}.\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)=\\sin ^{2}(ωx)-\\cos ^{2}(ωx)+2\\sqrt{3}\\sin (ωx)\\cos (ωx)$ $(ω > 0)$ is translated by a vector $\\vec{a}$ and satisfies the condition that all abscissas of the intersection points of the obtained graph with the coordinate axis form an arithmetic sequence.\n1. Find the value of $ω$ and the equation of $\\vec{a}$.\n2. Translate the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ by the following vector and then shift it upwards by $2$ units, resulting in the graph of $y=2\\sin (2x- \\frac{\\pi }{3})$. Determine the equation of the vector.\nTo find the value of \\(\\omega\\) and the equation of the vector \\(\\vec{a}\\), we first need to simplify the given function \\(f(x) = \\sin^2(\\omega x) - \\cos^2(\\omega x) + 2\\sqrt{3}\\sin(\\omega x)\\cos(\\omega x)\\).\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities \\(\\sin^2(\\theta) - \\cos^2(\\theta) = -\\cos(2\\theta)\\) and \\(2\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta) = \\sin(2\\theta)\\), we can rewrite the function as:\n\\[\nf(x) = -\\cos(2\\omega x) + \\sqrt{3}\\sin(2\\omega x).\n\\]\nWe can further simplify this using the angle addition formula. Notice that:\n\\[\n-\\cos(2\\omega x) + \\sqrt{3}\\sin(2\\omega x) = 2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\sin(2\\omega x) - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos(2\\omega x)\\right) = 2\\sin\\left(2\\omega x - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right).\n\\]\nThus, the function simplifies to:\n\\[\nf(x) = 2\\sin\\left(2\\omega x - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right).\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the value of \\(\\omega\\) such that the graph of \\(f(x)\\) after translation satisfies the condition that all abscissas of the intersection points of the obtained graph with the coordinate axis form an arithmetic sequence. This means that the graph overlaps either entirely or only once on the coordinate axes. Since the interval \\([a, b]\\) where the function overlaps the axes forms an arithmetic sequence, we know that the period of the function \\(f(x)\\) must be such that the function overlaps completely or only once. The period of \\(f(x) = 2\\sin\\left(2\\omega x - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\) is \\(\\pi/\\omega\\), and since it must match the period of a trigonometric function that overlaps completely or only once on the coordinate axes, \\(\\pi/\\omega = 2\\pi\\), so \\(\\omega = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nThe function now becomes:\n\\[\nf(x) = 2\\sin\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right).\n\\]\n\nThe vector \\(\\vec{a}\\) is the translation vector that the graph of \\(f(x)\\) undergoes. Since the function is only translated once and the parameter \\(\\omega = \\frac{1}{2}\\) implies a doubling of the distance traveled in half a period, we need to determine the translation distance. The period of \\(f(x)\\) is \\(\\pi\\), so the graph is translated by \\((\\pi, 0)\\) to satisfy the condition of the abscissas of the intersection points forming an arithmetic sequence.\n\nTherefore, \\(\\omega = \\frac{1}{2}\\) and \\(\\vec{a} = (\\pi, 0)\\).\n\nNow, we translate the graph of \\(y = 2\\sin\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\) upwards by 2 units, resulting in the graph of \\(y = 2\\sin\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + 2\\). This is equivalent to the graph of \\(y = 2\\sin\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(x - \\pi) - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\).\n\nWe need to determine the vector representing this translation. The function \\(y = 2\\sin\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(x - \\pi) - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\) overlaps the \\(x\\) axis at \\(x = \\pi\\) and \\(x = \\pi + 4\\pi k\\) for integer \\(k\\). The smallest \\(x\\) values are at \\(x = \\pi\\) and \\(x = \\pi + 4\\pi k\\), which corresponds to a translation by \\((4\\pi, 0)\\).\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vector is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(4\\pi, 0)}\n\\]",
